  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nCold/occluded front will continue tracking across the region\nthrough this morning. Isolated showers will remain possible as\nthe front and an associated upper level disturbance passes.\nClouds will likely linger into this evening before clearing\ngradually develops later tonight, with mostly sunny and cooler\nconditions expected Thursday.\n\nA strong upper level trough and associated cold front will\napproach from the Great Lakes region Thursday night into Friday.\nLow pressure will develop along the front and track off the\nnorthern New England coast while intensifying by Friday night.\nThis will bring periods of rain to the late Thursday night and\ncontinuing through Friday. 01Z/14 NBM 24-hour probs for >1\"\nrainfall ending 2 AM Saturday are generally 60-85% along and\nnorth of I-90, and 30-60% to the south, suggesting a soaking\nrainfall for many areas, especially north of I-90. Similar probs\nfor >2\" rainfall during this same time period are lower,\ngenerally 10-30% for higher elevations north of I-90, and less\nthan 10% elsewhere. This rainfall looks to be another beneficial\nrainfall for much of the region, with flooding not expected at \nthis time. Some elevated instability could even allow for a few \nrumbles of thunder, especially areas south of I-90 Friday \nmorning through early afternoon.\n\nTemperatures turn chilly Friday night as the cold front passes \nthrough with most of the higher elevations falling below \nfreezing and valleys dropping into the mid 30s by daybreak\nSaturday. The upper level trough axis will be centered over the\nregion by Saturday morning with 850 hpa temps dropping down to \naround -6 to -10 C, which is well below normal for mid October.\nAs a result, daytime temps will only be in the 30s across the \nhigh terrain on Saturday, with 40s for valley areas. After a \ncold Saturday night, highs on Sunday will moderate somewhat, but\nwill still be below normal. In addition, a strong pressure \ngradient will result in breezy to gusty conditions, making it \nfeel even colder through the weekend and making for a blustery \nlate fall day. Steep low level lapse rates amid strong pressure\nrises could mix down strong wind gusts on Saturday. \nProbabilistic guidance has a 30-60% chance for gusts exceeding \n40 mph within portions of the Mohawk Valley, Capital Region, \nBerkshires and eastern Catskills late Friday night through early\nSaturday afternoon which could be close to wind advisory range \nand knock down foliated trees and blow around any unsecured \noutdoors objects.\n\nAlthough moisture will be limited behind the cold front, some lake \nenhanced and upslope precip may occur behind the front, allowing for \nsome snow showers for western, northern and high terrain areas early \nSaturday morning. While some snowflakes do look likely across the \nhigher terrain this weekend, total amounts look very minor at this \ntime. Also, can not rule out some flurries extending into the\neastern Mohawk Valley and Capital Region Saturday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nGenerally dry weather and moderating temperatures are expected\nearly next week as high pressure builds across the region.\nDaytime highs should reach the lower/mid 60s in valley areas\nwith 50s for higher terrain.",
